          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should you reseal stamped concrete?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Acrylic sealers on stamped concrete typically need reapplication every two to three years on patios with moderate foot traffic, and every one to two years on driveways that see vehicle traffic. Polyurethane topcoats last four to seven years. Penetrating sealers last ten years or more. The simplest test is the water droplet method: sprinkle water on the surface. If it beads up and sits on top, the sealer is still working. If it soaks into the concrete and darkens the surface, it is time to reseal.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use glossy or matte sealer on stamped concrete?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              High-gloss sealers provide the most dramatic color enhancement and make stamped patterns pop visually, but they can look artificial on rough-textured patterns like fieldstone or cobblestone. Satin or semi-gloss sealers offer a more natural stone-like appearance while still improving color depth. For smooth stamped patterns like ashlar slate and large-format tile, high gloss looks excellent. For patterns mimicking rough natural stone, satin is usually more authentic and visually convincing.
            </p>
